# Arc in Rust

Atomic [Reference Counting](../../../../coding/concurrency/reference-counting/) provides reference counting with a guarantee that any increment or decrement operation is [atomic](../../../../coding/concurrency/atomic-operations/), meaning no two [threads](../../../../coding/concurrency/threads/) can simultaneously mutate the count.

This allows the reference-counted pointer to be safely shared and sent across threads.

`std::sync::Arc`: `Arc` stands for Atomically Reference Counted; it's the same as `Rc`, with a guarantee that any increment/decrement operation on `Arc` is [atomic](../../../../coding/concurrency/atomic-operations/), i.e., no two [threads](../../../../coding/concurrency/threads/) can simultaneously mutate the count.

To get the count of references, use `std::sync::Arc::strong_count` and `std::sync::Arc::get_mut` to get a mutable reference.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;

fn main() {
	let mut num = Arc::new(45);
	if let Some(val) = Arc::get_mut(&mut num) {
		*val = 42;
	}

	println!("{num:?}");
}
```
